**Read:** [Advanced Igneous Petrology — Timothy Grove, MIT 12.490](../sources/MIT-Advanced-Igneous-Petrology.lingua#r_3R0PJP97ZPE1RSGPPGY8WXXQZF), the full selected natural-system case and original papers; [Petrology — Dexter Perkins, open textbook](../sources/Petrology-Perkins.lingua#r_6P7JBVJJ3P1BT6QKDGC7BN6VWV), the complete relevant mineral/textural history treatment; [Introduction to Geochronology — Pieter Vermeesch](../../geology/sources/Introduction-to-Geochronology-Vermeesch.lingua#r_30AEAXYK3VV89FDX1TF9ZKJ84J), the complete chronological method relevant to the chosen domains.

An igneous sample can assemble crystals and melt with different histories of growth, storage, transport, resorption, and re-equilibration. Distinguish the final rock, a magma reservoir model, and the particular domain sampled by an analysis. One apparent temperature or age need not represent the whole history.

Zoning, inclusions, crystal-size distributions, trace/major chemistry, isotopes, and spatial relationships constrain different processes and timescales. Diffusion modifies profiles according to species, direction, conditions, boundary/initial states, and changing temperature. A simple √(Dt) scale is not a full time inversion.

Thermal/physical models should respect material and energy budgets and the phase behavior of a crystal-rich mixture. A proposed storage volume or long-lived melt body needs evidence beyond the existence of old crystal cores. Several intermittent assembly histories can preserve overlapping signals.

**Work:** reconstruct a complete published storage/history argument from raw domain context through analyses and models. Compare at least two interpretations of a zoned mineral population, testing one independent observation. Explain which age, diffusion, or thermal constraint measures an event, duration, bound, or model-dependent timescale.

Read the full advanced igneous case and primary methods papers. The later independent project requires an alternative interpretation and sensitivity to boundary conditions, not merely reproducing the preferred model's plot.

